  "grammarExplanation": "Direct Object Pronouns replace the noun receiving the action directly (what or who).\n\n• le = it/him (masculine)\n• la = it/her (feminine)\n• l' = it/him/her (before a vowel)\n• les = them (plural)\n\nIn French, they always go BEFORE the verb, even with infinitives:\n• 'Je vois le livre' → 'Je le vois'\n• 'Je vais lire le livre' → 'Je vais le lire'\nNote: In the passé composé, the past participle must agree with a preceding direct object. ('Je l'ai ouverte' = I opened it [la porte]).",
  "vocabulary": {
    "livre": {
      "mnemonic": "Think 'library' — full of livres (books)!",
      "explanation": "Means 'book'. Masculine noun."
    },
    "le": {
      "mnemonic": "The (masculine), but also 'him' or 'it'.",
      "explanation": "Masculine direct object pronoun."
    },
    "la": {
      "mnemonic": "The (feminine), but also 'her' or 'it'.",
      "explanation": "Feminine direct object pronoun."
    },
    "l'": {
      "mnemonic": "Smashed 'le' or 'la' before a vowel.",
      "explanation": "Used before vowels or silent H."
    },
    "les": {
